Modifications
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The vendored files have been modified from their upstream form, as required by
section 4(b) of the License. Every modification is produced mechanically by
`scripts/ai-elements-transforms.mjs` in the source repository, and the rules
that fired on a given file are listed in that file's header comment. As of this
release the layer is five rules, and they are, in full:

  - workspace-alias      Upstream imports its shadcn/ui primitives through the
                         `@repo/shadcn-ui` workspace package name. Both tiers
                         are vendored into this one published package, so those
                         specifiers are rewritten to paths within it.

  - relative-extensions  Relative import specifiers gain a `.js` extension, so
                         the emitted `dist/` resolves under Node's ESM resolver
                         rather than only under a bundler's.

  - a11y-touch-targets   Class strings and JSX attributes on interactive
                         elements are amended to meet the design system's
                         touch-target contracts: a `var(--target)` (44x44)
                         minimum hit area on primary controls, and a
                         `data-touch-target="dense"` classification on
                         deliberately compact inline affordances, which are held
                         to `var(--target-min)` (24x24, WCAG 2.2 AA SC 2.5.8)
                         instead. No colour, copy or behaviour is changed. The
                         controls affected, and the reason each was classified
                         one way rather than the other, are listed in
                         `scripts/ai-elements-patches/a11y.mjs` in the source
                         repository.

  - reduced-motion       One component's scroll default is changed from a
                         smooth animation to an instant jump. `Conversation`
                         in `src/components/conversation.tsx` is a
                         `role="log"` that scrolls itself on first paint and
                         on every content resize, and nothing in the vendored
                         tree guards that animation with
                         `prefers-reduced-motion`; an instant jump has no
                         motion to reduce. `{...props}` is spread last
                         upstream, so a consumer who wants the animation back
                         passes `initial="smooth"` — the default changes, the
                         API does not. No colour, copy, structure or geometry
                         is changed. The patched attributes, and the reason
                         this is a rule of its own rather than part of the one
                         above, are recorded in
                         `scripts/ai-elements-patches/motion.mjs` in the
                         source repository.

  - token-skin           Class names inside string literals are rewritten from
                         literal values to design system tokens. Two kinds:
                         Tailwind's own palette colours (`text-zinc-400`,
                         `bg-red-100 dark:bg-red-900/30`, `text-white`,
                         `bg-black/50`) and one literal radius, which no theme
                         variable can re-point; and shadcn/ui's `--accent`,
                         whose meaning upstream (a subtle hover tint) differs
                         from its meaning here (the brand signal colour), so
                         `bg-accent` becomes `bg-surface-2`. Where a light
                         value and its `dark:` counterpart both map to the same
                         token, the pair collapses to one class, since a token
                         already carries both themes. No behaviour, structure,
                         geometry or API is changed.

No other change is made to the upstream source. Apart from what these five
rules rewrite, and the generated provenance header at the top of each file, the
file contents are byte-identical to the pinned upstream release.

